From 5cbef394d51fa9347bde05d0a85ff0881bd440fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 14:00:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/18] KLZH (codec 29): single-table parse at level 1, repeat
 offsets after each match, no __memcpy_chk in the hot loops

- level 1: one table of 6-byte keys with the most recent offset tried one byte ahead (zstd-fast-like); levels 2-3
  (two tables) and level 1 try the second most recent offset right after each match, repeatedly (structured data
  alternates two offsets). zstd -3 against KLZH3 on Silesia + structured: +5.4% -> +2.1%.
- KLZ and KLZH copies use __builtin_memcpy: under _FORTIFY_SOURCE=3 (Ubuntu's default) a 16-byte memcpy into a
  buffer of run-time known size was a call to __memcpy_chk (10% of KLZH's encoder instructions; +13% compress).
- the sequence loop copies literals 16 bytes at a time and writes the bit buffer without a test per field.
- the fast parse grows its step every 128 literals (was 64): -0.2% size, same speed.

Formats unchanged (the parse only).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

(cherry picked from commit d9e86fa5 of the unmerged wave-4 LZ lane; review40/R01, claim
klz-w4-compat. The parse call sits in kzh_parse, which codecs 29 and 32 share, so KLZH2
codes the same wave-4 parse. FLAG: under KOLMO_NATIVE=lz codec 29 now writes other bytes
than rc3 did (same format, read by rc3): the test that pinned KLZH's bytes to rc3's is
replaced by blocks written by rc3 that must decode (tests/klz_rc3_frames.h); codec 28's
writer is still pinned. No default output changes.)
